<p>Maybe you’ve already seen Christopher Nolan’s new cinematic interpretation of the <em>Odyssey&nbsp;</em>more than once; maybe you don’t know whether you’ll ever get around to it at all. Either way, you’ve got to admit that the man makes films in a way very few living directors do. His combination of blockbuster-scale spectacle and personal vision, a rare experience indeed at the theater these days, can really only be achieved by filmmakers with a thoroughgoing love of the medium. Most of us will require no further proof of that love than <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t9fgFt-Ibik">this new video capturing his visit to the Criterion Closet</a>, that both hallowed and much-YouTubed space jam-packed with releases from the most widely known home-video brand for cinephiles.</p>
<p>Unsurprisingly, it’s not his first visit. “Last time I was here, there were no cameras, and I got to just come and steal some films,” he says, “but now I’m singing for my supper.” Unlike some filmmakers, he doesn’t play it cool for the audience; rather, he immediately starts pulling down&nbsp;Don Siegel’s neo-noir Hemingway adaptation&nbsp;<em>The Killers</em>, a five-picture John Cassavetes box set, and Al Reinert’s Brian Eno-scored NASA documentary <em>For All Mankind</em>. It’s not a surprise to see him go for an Orson Welles movie, but&nbsp;the one that first catches his eye is&nbsp;<em>Mr. Arkadin</em>, which Welles “made all over the world in the scrappiest, most sort of homemade, put-his-friends-together, grab-some-costumes, find-an-interesting-location kind of way.”</p>
