<p>Fritz Lang’s&nbsp;<em>The Testament of Dr. Mabuse</em>, he recalls, provided him and his brother-collaborator inspiration for the Joker as famously portrayed by Heath Ledger in <em>The Dark Knight</em>. The complete Apu trilogy by Satyajit Ray reminds him that, however great an impact the first of those films made on him, he never did watch the other two. For many moviegoers in the English-speaking world of the mid-twentieth century, Ray’s work was some of the first “world cinema” they’d ever seen; Nolan’s fellow cinephile-auteur Martin Scorsese has done his part to bolster that tradition with his World Cinema Project series, and Nolan pulls off the shelf one of its volumes featuring pictures from Cuba, Indonesia, Iran, and elsewhere.</p>
<p>Though technically British, Nolan is the partially Chicago-raised son of an American mother long since accepted as a kind of Hollywood native. His taste in movies, too, reflects a streak of high Americanism, which manifests in selections like Spike Lee’s&nbsp;<em>Malcolm X</em>, David Lynch’s <em>Lost Highway</em>, Carl Franklin’s&nbsp;<em>One False Move</em>,&nbsp;and Richard Linklater’s&nbsp;<em>Boyhood</em>. (As for Robert Altman’s satirical thriller <em>The Player</em>, it would be hard to imagine how he couldn’t appreciate its wickedly incisive depiction of&nbsp;“the Industry.”) Coming to the end of his Closet time, he also manages to grab Godfrey Reggio’s <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2012/02/koyaanisqatsi_at_1552_speed.html">Qatsi</a> trilogy, which constitutes “one of the great pieces of pure cinema,” and Carroll Ballard’s <em>The Black Stallion</em>, a childhood favorite that stayed with him. Like all the most successful filmmakers, Nolan understands that you’ve got to end strong, and in a way that doesn’t require explanation: hence, perhaps, his final choice of&nbsp;<em>Citizen Kane</em>.</p>
